        <div id="what-is-mps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Muscle Protein Synthesis Is (and What It Isn't)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Muscle is never static. Your body is constantly building new muscle protein, a process called muscle protein synthesis, and breaking old protein down, called muscle protein breakdown. Whether you gain, keep or lose muscle over weeks and months depends on the balance between the two, known as net protein balance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two things push that balance toward growth. Resistance exercise makes muscle more sensitive to building, and that heightened sensitivity lasts at least 24 hours, possibly up to 48 hours in less trained people. Eating protein supplies the amino acids that synthesis needs and directly switches it on. Training plus protein produces a bigger and longer positive balance than either alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Researchers measure muscle protein synthesis with stable isotope tracers and muscle biopsies, usually over a few hours after a single meal or drink. That is where most of the numbers in this article come from. It is precise, controlled and useful, but it is also a snapshot. A supplement that raises synthesis for three hours in a lab does not automatically build more muscle over three months.
          </p>

          <p>
            Researchers have shown that gap directly. Work by Mitchell and colleagues in 2014 found that the size of the acute synthesis response after a training session did not predict how much muscle people gained over the following weeks, and work by Damas and colleagues suggested that early in a program much of the elevated synthesis goes toward repairing damage rather than growth. So when a product cites a short-term synthesis study, ask whether long-term training trials agree.
          </p>

          <p>
            With that caveat in place, the acute studies are still the best tool for answering practical questions such as how much protein to eat at once, which proteins work fastest, and whether isolated amino acids can stand in for a full protein. For whether protein supplements build muscle over months, our review of <a href="/do-protein-supplements-build-muscle/">whether protein supplements build muscle</a> covers the long-term trials.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="leucine-threshold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Leucine Threshold
          </h2>

          <p>
            Of the nine essential amino acids, leucine plays a special role. Besides being a building block, it acts as a signal: rising leucine in the blood activates a pathway called mTORC1, which tells muscle cells to start making protein. This is the basis of the leucine trigger idea, which holds that a meal needs to raise blood leucine past a certain point to fully switch on synthesis.
          </p>

          <p>
            For young adults that point appears to be roughly 2 to 3 grams of leucine in a meal. A 25-gram serving of whey provides about 3 grams, which is one reason that dose became the standard in research. Older adults appear to need more, which is part of what researchers call anabolic resistance, the blunted response of aging muscle to protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 2014 study by Churchward-Venne and colleagues in the American Journal of Clinical Nutrition shows how strong the leucine signal is. Young men drank either 25 grams of whey or just 6.25 grams of whey with extra leucine bringing the total to 5 grams. After exercise, the small leucine-boosted drink raised synthesis to rates similar to the full 25 grams, over the hours measured.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not mean leucine alone is enough. Synthesis needs every essential amino acid as raw material, and leucine can only pull the trigger if the other building blocks are available. Leucine by itself runs out of material quickly. The practical takeaway is not to buy leucine pills, but to make sure each protein-containing meal is big enough and good enough to supply 2 to 3 grams of leucine naturally.
          </p>

          <p>
            Long-term data supports that view. In a 2009 trial in the American Journal of Clinical Nutrition, Verhoeven and colleagues gave healthy older men 2.5 grams of leucine with each main meal for 12 weeks. Muscle mass and strength did not improve. Adding leucine to an already adequate diet did not build muscle, which is why we rank standalone leucine supplements low.
          </p>

