        <div id="dose-per-meal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Protein per Meal Maximizes Synthesis
          </h2>

          <p>
            For years the standard answer was 20 to 25 grams. That figure came from studies such as Moore and colleagues in 2009, who found synthesis after leg exercise plateaued at around 20 grams of egg protein, and Witard and colleagues in 2014, who gave 0, 10, 20 or 40 grams of whey after a single-leg workout. In the Witard study, 20 grams raised synthesis by about 49 percent over no protein and 40 grams by about 56 percent, while the extra protein mostly increased amino acid burning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Later studies complicated the picture. In 2016 Macnaughton and colleagues used a whole-body workout instead of one leg and found 40 grams of whey produced about 20 percent higher synthesis than 20 grams. More muscle trained appears to mean more demand for amino acids. Then in 2023 Trommelen and colleagues, writing in Cell Reports Medicine, gave 100 grams versus 25 grams of protein after whole-body exercise and found the larger dose produced a greater and longer anabolic response, lasting more than 12 hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            The idea that your body can only use 20 to 30 grams at once is therefore wrong as stated. Larger meals still get used; they simply produce a longer, lower-efficiency response. That said, the Trommelen study compared just two doses in a lab, and nobody has shown that one huge protein meal builds more muscle over months than the same total spread out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Age shifts the target. A pooled analysis by Moore and colleagues in 2015 estimated that synthesis plateaued at about 0.24 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per meal in young men and about 0.40 grams per kilogram in older men. A 2018 paper by Schoenfeld and Aragon recommended about 0.4 grams per kilogram per meal across at least four meals to reach a daily total of 1.6 grams per kilogram, going up to about 0.55 grams per kilogram per meal for those aiming higher.
          </p>

          <p>
            For an 80-kilogram lifter, that works out to roughly 30 to 45 grams of protein per meal, four times a day. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> runs the numbers for your weight, and our guide to <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> covers how to space the meals. Older adults should lean toward the higher per-meal figure; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-seniors/">protein powder for seniors</a> guide covers options, and anyone with kidney disease should check intake with their doctor.
          </p>

