              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0.40 g/kg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Plateau, older men (Moore 2015)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0.4 g/kg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per meal, 4+ meals (Schoenfeld and Aragon)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">12+ h</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Response to 100 g (Trommelen 2023)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="whey-eaa-bcaa" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Whey vs EAAs vs BCAAs
          </h2>

          <p>
            BCAAs, the branched-chain amino acids leucine, isoleucine and valine, are the most heavily marketed muscle protein synthesis supplement and the least justified. The problem is simple: they are three of the nine essential amino acids. Building muscle protein needs all nine, so a BCAA drink can send the signal to build while supplying only a third of the material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Robert Wolfe laid out the case in a 2017 review in the Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition. He calculated that BCAAs alone could raise synthesis by about 30 percent at the very most, and only by drawing on amino acids freed from muscle breakdown. He found no human studies at the time that had measured the response to oral BCAAs alone, and two intravenous studies in which BCAAs actually lowered synthesis. He concluded the anabolic claim for BCAAs alone was unwarranted.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same year, Jackman and colleagues published the first oral test, in Frontiers in Physiology. Ten trained men drank 5.6 grams of BCAAs or a placebo after leg exercise. Myofibrillar synthesis was 22 percent higher with BCAAs. That is a real effect, but the authors noted it was roughly half the response earlier studies had recorded with a whey dose containing a similar amount of BCAAs. Whey does the same job better, and costs less per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Essential amino acid supplements, usually sold as EAAs, are a more sensible product because they contain all nine. Free-form EAAs are absorbed quickly and can stimulate synthesis at small doses. But whey is itself about 43 percent essential amino acids, according to the 2018 Gorissen analysis, so 25 grams of whey supplies roughly 11 grams of EAAs plus the rest of a complete protein, usually at a lower price per gram of EAAs than a dedicated EAA powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is little long-term evidence that EAAs build more muscle than an equivalent amount of whey or food protein. Where EAAs make sense is convenience: a low-calorie drink during long training sessions, a supplement for someone who cannot tolerate dairy and dislikes plant powders, or a way to raise the essential amino acid content of a lower-quality meal. For most people, a whole protein is the better buy. Our <a href="/highest-quality-protein/">protein quality guide</a> explains why complete proteins score best.
          </p>

