          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="creatine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Creatine: Works, but Not by Raising Synthesis
          </h2>

          <p>
            Creatine monohydrate is the best-supported muscle-building supplement after protein itself. The International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand on creatine, published in 2017, describes it as the most effective nutritional supplement available for increasing high-intensity exercise capacity and lean body mass during training, with an extensive safety record in healthy people.
          </p>

          <p>
            Interestingly, creatine does not appear to build muscle by directly increasing muscle protein synthesis. In a 2003 study in the American Journal of Physiology, Louis and colleagues found that creatine supplementation had no effect on myofibrillar or sarcoplasmic protein synthesis after resistance exercise. Earlier work from the same group found no effect on muscle protein turnover at rest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Instead, creatine raises the stores of phosphocreatine in muscle, which regenerates energy for short, hard efforts. That lets you squeeze out an extra rep or two per set, and over weeks that additional training volume translates into more muscle and strength. Creatine also draws water into muscle cells, so some of the early weight gain, often a kilogram or so in the first week of a loading phase, is water rather than tissue.
          </p>

          <p>
            The standard dose is 3 to 5 grams of creatine monohydrate a day. An optional loading phase of about 0.3 grams per kilogram per day for five to seven days saturates muscle faster, but daily low doses reach the same point in three to four weeks. Timing barely matters, so take it whenever you will remember. Monohydrate is the form with the evidence; more expensive forms have not been shown to work better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Creatine can raise blood creatinine, a marker used to estimate kidney function, without indicating kidney damage, so tell your doctor you take it before blood work. People with kidney disease or on medication that affects the kidneys should talk to their doctor before starting. Look for a product with third-party testing, as with any supplement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hmb" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. HMB: Small Effects for Specific People
          </h2>

          <p>
            HMB, short for beta-hydroxy beta-methylbutyrate, is a compound your body makes from leucine; only a small fraction of the leucine you eat is converted to it. It is marketed as a way to boost synthesis and reduce muscle breakdown. The mechanism is plausible: a small acute study by Wilkinson and colleagues in 2013 found that a single dose of HMB raised muscle protein synthesis and reduced breakdown.
          </p>

          <p>
            The long-term results in trained people are disappointing. A 2018 meta-analysis by Sanchez-Martinez and colleagues in the Journal of Science and Medicine in Sport pooled six randomized trials with 193 trained athletes and found trivial, non-significant effects on bench press, leg press, body mass, fat-free mass and fat mass. The results held across doses, durations and training levels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Older analyses found small benefits in untrained people starting a training program, mostly in leg strength. Some of the most impressive positive studies in trained lifters have been questioned by other researchers for results that were far larger than anything replicated since, so treat dramatic claims with caution.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where HMB may be more useful is in preserving muscle during inactivity in older adults. A 2013 study by Deutz and colleagues found that HMB helped older adults retain lean mass during ten days of bed rest. If you are older, facing a period of immobility, and already eating enough protein, HMB at the usual 3 grams a day is a reasonable conversation to have with your doctor. For a healthy, trained lifter eating enough protein, it is unlikely to be worth the money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip-list" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What's Not Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            BCAAs, if you already eat enough protein. Every protein-containing meal and every scoop of whey already supplies a generous dose of BCAAs, along with the six essential amino acids BCAAs lack. Adding more on top does nothing measurable for muscle. The same applies to standalone leucine; the Verhoeven trial showed no gain from extra leucine on top of an adequate diet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Glutamine. It is the most abundant amino acid in muscle, which made it a logical candidate, but training studies in healthy adults, including a 2001 trial by Candow and colleagues, found no added effect on muscle size or strength when glutamine was taken during resistance training. It may have clinical uses in specific medical settings, but not for building muscle in healthy people.
          </p>

          <p>
            Testosterone boosters and anabolic blends. Products built around tribulus, fenugreek, D-aspartic acid and similar herbs have not shown meaningful increases in muscle in healthy men, and proprietary blends hide the doses, so you cannot tell how much of anything you are getting. Some supplements in this category have been found to contain undeclared drugs, which is a serious safety risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein powders that rely on amino spiking. Some products add cheap free amino acids such as glycine, taurine or creatine to raise the protein number on the label without adding real protein. Those additions do nothing for muscle protein synthesis. Check that the protein grams come from whole protein sources, and prefer products with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice certification, which verify the label. Our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">clean protein powder</a> guide covers how to check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="simple-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. A Simple, Evidence-Based Plan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with a daily protein target of about 1.6 grams per kilogram of body weight, up to 2.2 if you are dieting or want to be sure. Split it across three to five meals with roughly 0.3 to 0.4 grams per kilogram each, or 0.4 and above if you are older. Use protein powder to fill the meals that fall short, which for most people is breakfast or a snack.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose the protein by tolerance and budget. Whey concentrate gives the most leucine per dollar; isolate suits people sensitive to lactose; casein works before bed; plant blends work if dosed a little higher. Our guide to the <a href="/types-of-protein-powder/">types of protein powder</a> compares them, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-muscle-gain/">best protein powder for muscle gain</a> picks list tested products for each type.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add 3 to 5 grams of creatine monohydrate a day. That is the one supplement beyond protein with consistent long-term evidence for more muscle and strength. Skip BCAAs, glutamine and test boosters, and consider HMB only in the specific situations above. Everything else on the shelf is optional at best.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of it replaces the basics. Progressive resistance training is what makes muscle responsive to protein in the first place, and enough sleep and total calories determine how much of that response turns into growth. Supplements fine-tune a good program; they cannot rescue a missing one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying BCAAs instead of protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                BCAAs supply only three of the nine essential amino acids, so they signal muscle building without providing enough material. Jackman's 2017 study found a response roughly half that of whey with similar BCAAs. If budget is limited, whey or a complete plant blend is the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking tiny doses of plant protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 20-gram scoop of pea protein supplies noticeably less leucine than 20 grams of whey. If you use plant protein, scale the serving to about 30 to 35 grams, or choose a blend, so each meal clears the leucine threshold that switches on synthesis.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting creatine to work instantly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without a loading phase, creatine takes three to four weeks to saturate muscle. The early scale jump is mostly water. Judge it by strength and training volume over two to three months, not by the first week's weight.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting short-term synthesis studies alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A product that raises synthesis for three hours in a lab has not proven it builds more muscle over months. Acute synthesis does not reliably predict long-term growth. Look for training trials lasting eight weeks or more before paying a premium.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underdosing protein as you age</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Older muscle needs roughly 0.4 grams of protein per kilogram per meal to reach peak synthesis, compared with about 0.24 in young men. A 20-gram shake that works for a 25-year-old may fall short at 70. Check with a doctor first if you have kidney disease.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What supplement increases muscle protein synthesis the most?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Protein itself, particularly a leucine-rich, fast-digesting protein such as whey. About 25 to 40 grams per meal raises synthesis sharply, and larger doses extend the response. Essential amino acid supplements can also raise synthesis, but they rarely beat whey on value. Creatine builds muscle over time but does not appear to raise synthesis directly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much leucine do I need per meal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Research suggests roughly 2 to 3 grams of leucine per meal maximizes muscle protein synthesis in young adults, with older adults needing somewhat more. About 25 grams of whey supplies around 3 grams. Plant proteins typically contain less leucine per gram, so a 30 to 35 gram serving is a sensible target for pea or soy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are EAAs better than BCAAs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. EAAs contain all nine essential amino acids, while BCAAs contain only three. Muscle protein synthesis requires all nine, so EAAs can support a full response while BCAAs cannot. For most people, though, whey or another complete protein supplies the same essential amino acids at a lower cost, with more long-term evidence behind it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do BCAAs do anything at all?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They produce a modest effect. A 2017 study found 5.6 grams of BCAAs raised muscle protein synthesis about 22 percent compared with placebo after training, but that was roughly half the response seen with whey containing similar BCAAs. If you already eat enough protein, extra BCAAs are unlikely to add anything measurable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does creatine increase muscle protein synthesis?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not directly. A 2003 study found creatine supplementation had no effect on muscle protein synthesis after resistance exercise. Creatine works by increasing phosphocreatine stores, which lets you train harder and accumulate more volume over time. That extra training is what produces the additional muscle and strength seen in long-term studies.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is HMB worth taking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For healthy, trained lifters, probably not. A 2018 meta-analysis of trained athletes found trivial, non-significant effects on strength and body composition. HMB may help untrained beginners slightly and may help older adults retain muscle during bed rest. If you are in that group, discuss it with your doctor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you take too much protein at once?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in the sense that it is wasted. A 2023 study found 100 grams of protein produced a larger and longer anabolic response than 25 grams. Bigger meals are used less efficiently, and spreading protein across three to five meals remains a sensible default. People with kidney disease should follow medical advice on total intake.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

